    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Tokens in the Browser with Spring Boot: a BFF and DPoP',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains B F F and DPoP with Spring, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A single-page app that keeps tokens in localStorage hands them to every script on the page, and a stolen refresh token is a long visit; a Spring Boot backend for the frontend keeps the tokens in its own session store and gives the browser a __Host- cookie and an X S R F cookie, refreshes the access token on the server, and refuses requests that another site started; and where a token must reach the browser, DPoP binds it to a key, so Spring Security's resource server refuses it without a fresh proof, though a script on the open page can still ask the key for one. [[slnc 600]] Think of it this way. [[slnc 300]] A hotel safe against a note in your pocket. The B F F is the safe at the front desk: the key card in your pocket opens nothing by itself and is useless anywhere else. DPoP is a key card that only works with the ring on your finger. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online bank. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will be able to: [[slnc 300]] Explain what a script can read in each design. [[slnc 300]] Build the cookie that a B F F gives the browser, and say why each attribute is there. [[slnc 300]] Explain what DPoP stops and what it does not."),
